+-- | Like 'mkUnivCoFromDeps', but establishes (UnivCoDepsInv) itself, adding the
+-- 'CoVar's and 'CoercionHole's of the LHS and RHS types to the dependencies.
+mkUnivCoAddTypeDeps :: HasDebugCallStack
+                    => UnivCoProvenance -> UnivCoDeps -> Role -> Type -> Type -> Coercion
+mkUnivCoAddTypeDeps prov deps role ty1 ty2
+  | ty1 `eqType` ty2 = mkReflCo role ty1
+  | otherwise
+  = let !deps' = addUnivCoTypeDeps [ty1, ty2] deps
+    in mk_univ_co prov deps' role ty1 ty2
+
+-- | Shared helper for 'mkUnivCoFromDeps' and 'mkUnivCoAddTypeDeps'.
+mk_univ_co :: HasDebugCallStack
+           => UnivCoProvenance -> UnivCoDeps -> Role -> Type -> Type -> Coercion
+mk_univ_co prov deps role ty1 ty2 =
+  -- Assert (UnivCoDepsInv) from Note [UnivCoDepsInv] in GHC.Core.TyCo.Rep.
+  assertPprMaybe (checkUnivCoDepsInv ty1 ty2 deps) $
+    UnivCo { uco_prov = prov, uco_role = role
+           , uco_lty = ty1, uco_rty = ty2
+           , uco_deps = deps }
+{-# INLINE mk_univ_co #-}
+
+-- | Checks (UnivCoDepsInv) from Note [UnivCoDepsInv] in GHC.Core.TyCo.Rep.
+--
+-- Returns an error message text in case of failure.
+checkUnivCoDepsInv :: Type -> Type -> UnivCoDeps -> Maybe SDoc
+checkUnivCoDepsInv ty1 ty2 deps
+  | isEmptyUnivCoDeps undeclared
+  = Nothing
+  | otherwise
+  = Just $ hang (text "UnivCo: incomplete dependencies") 2 $ vcat
+      [ text "undeclared:" <+> ppr undeclared
+      , text "       lhs:" <+> ppr ty1
+      , text "       rhs:" <+> ppr ty2
+      , text "      deps:" <+> ppr deps ]
+  where
+    undeclared = addUnivCoTypeDeps [ty1, ty2] emptyUnivCoDeps `minusUnivCoDeps` deps
+
+{- Note [Propagating zappedness]
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+mkTransCo collapses a composition involving a zapped coercion, thusly:
+
+  ZappedCo l m ; ZappedCo m r = ZappedCo l r
+  ZappedCo l m ; co2          = ZappedCo l (coercionRKind co2)
+  co1          ; ZappedCo m r = ZappedCo (coercionLKind co1) r
+
+This avoids accumulating large types in transitive chains involving zapped
+coercions.
+
+How do these chains arise? The simplifier (and the simple optimiser) merge
+nested casts using 'mkTransCo', without calling the coercion optimiser on the
+result (see Note [Avoid re-simplifying coercions] in GHC.Core.Opt.Simplify.Iteration).
+
+A typical example is T12227, which has a zapped type family reduction followed
+by newtype axioms involving large GHC.Generics types:
+
+  x |> ( ZappedCo (Rep Foo) T
+       ; N:M1 <..> <D> <md> <C1 mc T_inner>
+       ; N:M1 <..> <C> <mc> <T_inner> )
+
+This zaps to "x |> ZappedCo (Rep Foo) T_inner" which is much smaller.
+
+This is a performance heuristic more than anything else. Tests on which this
+makes a significant difference in coercion sizes:
+
+  T5321Fun, CoOpt_Singletons, T12227, T15703, T9630, T12707
+    - coercion sizes: -42% (geo. mean), ranging from -17% (T15703)
+      to -73% (T5321Fun)
+    - compile-time allocations: -1.3% (geo. mean), at most -3.9% (T12227)
+
+NB: the mixed cases (ZappedCo ; co2 and co1 ; ZappedCo) are the ones that
+matter. Only collapsing (ZappedCo ; ZappedCo) was measured to make no
+difference at all on the tests above (0.0% on every metric).
+
+One might expect that the extra calls to 'coercionLKind'/'coercionRKind' would
+cost runtime, but empirically this does not seem to be the case, perhaps because
+this optimisation prevents large chains involving ZappedCo from forming at all,
+so we never need to traverse a long transitive chain to compute 'coercionLKind'
+and 'coercionRKind'.
+
+Other smart constructors (such as mkInstCo, mkSymCo, mkSelCo) do not seem to
+benefit from propagating zappedness (0.0% change on perf/compiler).
+Note however that this only holds with the current design, detailed in
+Note [Coercion zapping] in GHC.Tc.Solver.Rewrite, in which only the coercions
+produced by the rewriter are zapped. If we were to zap more coercions, then
+propagating zappedness in 'mkInstCo' may become important. For example, MR !611
+(a prior attempt at coercion zapping) significantly regressed because it lacked
+this 'mkInstCo' optimisation, resulting in large coercions of the form
+
+   Nth (Nth (... (Inst univ_co_with_large_types arg)))
+-}
 
